---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base
  title="Australia"
  eyebrow="1826 — the arrival"
  dek="A major of the 39th Foot came out as a convict guard, and his children's birthplaces record the voyage: Chatham in 1825, Parramatta in 1827. The family becomes Australian in the space of two years."

>
  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">This is the part of the pedigree that does not need defending. Three birthplaces,
    read in order, tell the whole story — and a regimental history, written by strangers with no
    interest in this family, confirms it line for line.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Child of George Pitt D'Arcy</th><th class="m">Born</th><th>At</th><th>What the regiment was doing</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Charlotte Maria</strong></td><td class="m">1825</td>
            <td>Chatham, Kent</td>
            <td>In July 1825 the 39th marched to Cork, then to <strong>Chatham</strong>, to embark for
            New South Wales as guards over convicts.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Catherine Maria</strong></td><td class="m">1827</td>
            <td>Parramatta, New South Wales</td>
            <td>The regiment was in the colony from 1826, with detachments at
            <strong>Parramatta</strong>, Norfolk Island, Port Macquarie, Newcastle and Liverpool.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>George Lindsay</strong></td><td class="m">24 Aug 1829</td>
            <td>Parramatta, New South Wales</td>
            <td>Christened at <strong>St James's, Sydney</strong>, 5 November 1829. The direct
            ancestor. <a href={u("/direct-line")}>The spine →</a></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>Why this is strong evidence and not just a nice fit.</strong> The regimental record was
      not written about the D'Arcys and gains nothing from agreeing with them. Chatham in 1825 and
      Parramatta from 1826 is where the <em>39th (Dorsetshire) Regiment of Foot</em> was, whoever else
      happened to be there. A family whose children are born in exactly those two places in exactly
      those two years, whose father is recorded as <em>Major in the 39th Battalion of Foot</em>, is a
      family moving with that regiment.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The man</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Major George Pitt D'Arcy, 1783–1849</h2>
    <p>Born 18 February 1783 — at Portsmouth, or in the West Indies; the record disagrees with itself
    and both are plausible for an artillery family of that decade, since his younger siblings were
    born in <strong>Barbados</strong>. Third of the ten children of Major-General Robert D'Arcy of the
    Royal Engineers.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">He came to New South Wales in <strong>1826</strong> with the 39th. He
    never went home. He died at <strong>Parramatta on 22 July 1849</strong>, twenty-three years after
    he arrived and seventeen years after his regiment left for India without him.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.9rem" class="small">He married twice, and the tree is confused about which
    wife bore which child — <em>Maria or Mary Le Meassier</em> and <em>Maria (Catherine) White</em>,
    both marriages placed at Chatham in 1810. Two women, the same given name, the same year, the same
    town. This is flagged rather than resolved. <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open questions →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">His eldest son</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Frederick Robert D'Arcy — the surveyor who helped map Melbourne</h2>
    <p>Not in the direct line — he is <strong>George Lindsay's half-brother</strong> — but the most
    publicly documented member of the family, and the reason the D'Arcy name appears in Australian
    reference works at all.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">Born Chatham 1811 and christened there on 2 July, he was
    <em>withdrawn from Sandhurst by his family to emigrate</em>, and sailed for Sydney on the ship
    <strong>Albion</strong>, 3 November 1828. About 1830 he was taken on as a draughtsman in the New
    South Wales Surveyor-General's Department under <strong>Major Thomas Mitchell</strong>.</p>

    <blockquote>
      In late 1836 Governor Bourke sent three surveyors — Robert Russell, <strong>Frederick
      D'Arcy</strong> and William Darke — with thirty-six convicts to the Port Phillip District, to
      chart the shore of Port Phillip Bay and plot the course of the Yarra River.
    </blockquote>

    <p>That survey is the ground plan of <strong>Melbourne</strong>. He went on to work in the Blue
    Mountains, at Rylestone and Merriwa, and finally in Queensland; he was, by the account of his
    modern biographer, an explorer of rougher country than any other early mainland surveyor, and a
    man who "lurched from one disaster to another", embroiled in disputes and scandals in New South
    Wales, Port Phillip, Van Diemen's Land and Queensland.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">He died at <strong>Spring Hill, Brisbane, on 19 September 1875</strong>
    and was buried at <strong>Toowong</strong> the next day — in the same city, in the same decade, as
    the half-brother whose line this archive follows. <a href={u("/brisbane")}>Brisbane →</a></p>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1rem">He is the subject of a full-length biography:
    Andy Macqueen, <em>Frederick Robert D'Arcy: Colonial Surveyor, Explorer and Artist, c.1809–1875</em>.
    Note that Macqueen dates his birth to about 1809; the family tree says 1811 and the Chatham
    christening of 2 July 1811 supports the tree. The discrepancy is recorded, not resolved.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What this settles, and what it does not</h2>
    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Settled</h3>
        <p>This family reached Australia in 1826, as soldiers rather than as convicts or as free
        settlers, and it reached it from <strong>Chatham</strong> — the Royal Engineers town where
        George Pitt's father commanded. The arrival is military, and it is documented from two
        independent directions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Not settled</h3>
        <p>Everything above Major-General Robert D'Arcy at Chatham. The Australian story stands
        entirely on its own; it does not need the Yorkshire castle, and it does not prove it either.
        <a href={u("/hornby")}>The Hornby claim →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
